<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Did the Pelicans Extend Jordan Poole?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Poole is paid <strong>$34.04m</strong> because Golden State extended him at that price weeks after he had been a starter on a championship team, and the contract has followed him since.</p>
<ul>
<li>He was extended in October 2022 for <strong>$128m</strong> over four years.</li>
<li>His pay went from <strong>$3.9m</strong> to <strong>$27.46m</strong> when it started, and he had been traded by then.</li>
<li>The contract carries <strong>$17m</strong> of incentives, tied to awards he has not won.</li>
<li>He went 28th in 2019, so the rookie scale paid him only <strong>$10.09m</strong> across four years.</li>
<li>He has been traded twice inside this contract.</li>
</ul>
<p>He has earned <strong>$133.09m</strong> and <strong>$123m</strong> of it comes from one contract.</p>

</div><div class="bde-shortcode-506-159 bde-shortcode"><div class="nba-fin" data-nba-fin="why">
